        "title": "Axler and McGill 1997, low-back loads over a variety of abdominal exercises: searching for the safest abdominal challenge (Med Sci Sports Exerc 29(6):804-811)",
        "url": "https://acnpsearch.tweb-dev.unibo.it/singlejournalindex/7588015"
      }
    ],
    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG.",
        "Advanced: it combines a sit-up and a straight-leg raise, the two higher-compression options in McGill's comparison (McGill 1998).",
        "Low-back pain, disc problems or flexion intolerance: start with a McGill-style curl-up, dead bug or plank; partial curl-ups gave the best abdominal challenge for the least spine compression in one model study (Axler and McGill 1997).",
        "Pregnancy and postpartum (diastasis recti): get individual guidance before dynamic trunk-flexion or straight-leg work."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Bend the knees (tuck-up) or lift one leg at a time.",
        "Shorten the range: hands to the knees."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Tuck-up / crunch with knees in.",
        "Single-leg V-up (alternating).",
        "Crunch."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Slow 3 s lowering.",
        "V-sit hold at the top.",
        "Light weight held in the hands."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "core": "Rectus abdominis curls the trunk and holds the pelvis while the legs rise. No study measured the V-up itself; it joins a sit-up and a bilateral leg raise, and bilateral (not unilateral) leg lifts needed abdominal activation (Andersson 1997).",
      "hip_flexors_r": "Iliopsoas lifts the legs and the trunk at the same time: hip flexors were highly active in exercises that flex the hip by lifting either the upper body or the legs (Andersson 1997), and leg raises gave the highest psoas activity in McGill's data (McGill 1998).",
      "hip_flexors_l": "Iliopsoas lifts the legs and the trunk at the same time: hip flexors were highly active in exercises that flex the hip by lifting either the upper body or the legs (Andersson 1997), and leg raises gave the highest psoas activity in McGill's data (McGill 1998).",
      "obliques_r": "Assist trunk flexion and keep the balance on the hips steady.",
      "obliques_l": "Assist trunk flexion and keep the balance on the hips steady.",
      "quads_r": "Rectus femoris flexes the hips and keeps the knees straight; rectus femoris activity was higher in hip-flexion sit-ups than in leg lifts (Andersson 1997).",
      "quads_l": "Rectus femoris flexes the hips and keeps the knees straight; rectus femoris activity was higher in hip-flexion sit-ups than in leg lifts (Andersson 1997).",
      "adductors_r": "Keep the legs pressed together (clinical opinion).",
      "adductors_l": "Keep the legs pressed together (clinical opinion)."
    }
  },
